2. Install the Python tools for A.D.A.
Install the basic dependencies:
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip unzip git


Create a project folder and Python virtual environment:
mkdir -p ~/Cyberdeck
cd ~/Cyberdeck
python3 -m venv ada-env
source ada-env/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip


Whenever you want to work on A.D.A. later, activate the environment with:
cd ~/Cyberdeck
source ada-env/bin/activate


3. Extract your A.D.A. project
Copy your ada_local-main.zip archive onto the Pi, then locate it. If you saved it in your Downloads folder, run:
ls ~/Downloads


Extract it into the Cyberdeck directory:
mkdir -p ~/Cyberdeck/ada
unzip ~/Downloads/ada_local-main.zip -d ~/Cyberdeck/ada


If the ZIP is somewhere else, replace ~/Downloads/ada_local-main.zip with its actual path.
Inspect the extracted files:
cd ~/Cyberdeck/ada
find . -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort | head -200


Look for project instructions and dependency files:
find . -maxdepth 3 -type f \( -name "README*" -o -name "requirements.txt" -o -name "pyproject.toml" -o -name "environment.yml" \)


If the project contains a requirements.txt, install its listed dependencies inside the activated virtual environment:
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt


Run that command from the directory containing the file. If the project has a pyproject.toml instead, follow its installation instructions.
